 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+)

diff --git a/docs/baseline/pinning.md b/docs/baseline/pinning.md
index fe2357e6..fb49bdad 100644
--- a/docs/baseline/pinning.md
+++ b/docs/baseline/pinning.md
@@ -78,5 +78,12 @@ update the `agents.lock` entry -> re-derive the schemas -> re-record through CI
 2. Store location on CI (`actions/cache` keyed by lock hash, or re-download each run). Default: re-download and verify each run (a cache is another trust root).
 3. Who updates the lock: a scheduled drift job opens a PR that bumps entries, or a human edits. Default: the job reports; a human bumps.
 
+4. The live API keys reach GitHub Actions. "GitHub-hosted runners only" and "the API proxy holds the key as a separate user or service container" both mean the real keys live in GitHub:
+   as Environment secrets, one environment per agent (`live-<agent>`). Anyone who can run, or edit a workflow that declares, that environment can read that agent's key (and an environment's
+   protection rules, a required reviewer or a branch restriction, are what narrow that). This WIDENS the credential surface compared with keys that exist only in 1Password on Bobby's machine:
+   Bobby decides whether it is acceptable. Default: yes for the toolpath-ci keys only (they are capped: claude 10 USD/month; codex and pi have no provider-side cap, so the proxy's caps are the
+   only limit), with a required reviewer on each `live-<agent>` environment.
+5. `workflow_dispatch` only means a human click per live run. That click is the cap on spend: nothing schedules a live call. A scheduled job would be the drift REPORT (no keys), never a capture.
+
 ## Not run
 Design only; no code, no gates.
-- 
